derivations.claim:
Suppose $u\in C^2(\overline\Omega)$ is real, $-\Delta u=f$ and $\partial_
u u=g$ on $\partial\Omega$. By [F7] the field $
abla u$ lies in $C^1(\overline\Omega;\mathbb R^n)$, so the divergence theorem [F3] applies to it: $\int_\Omega\Delta u\,dx=\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}
abla u\,dx=\int_{\partial\Omega}
abla u\cdot
u\,dS=\int_{\partial\Omega}g\,dS$, the last step by [F3] and the definition of $g$. Since $\Delta u=-f$ pointwise, this is $-\int_\Omega f=\int_{\partial\Omega}g$, that is, $\int_\Omega f=-\int_{\partial\Omega}g\,dS$, the compatibility equation of clause 3.
